-### Neural Network Multiple Scattering Representation
-Uses neural networks instead of tables to represent multiple scattering, reducing memory usage from hundreds of MB to kB without significant reduction in quality [S1]. It maps a seven-dimensional function (anisotropy $g$, albedo $\alpha$, location $r(\rho, z)$, direction $\theta, \phi$) to radiance via a four-layer neural network [S1]. During rendering, the network reproduces the precomputed table for a specific medium, which is then used for multiple scattering computation [S1]. Storage is reduced from 50 GB to 23.6 kB [S1].
+Computes the radiative transfer equation for $K$ scattering orders via integral precomputation. Choose when explicit control over scattering orders is required rather than a black-box table lookup.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Precomputation time is approx. 1 minute for high-albedo materials [S2]. Rendering time is significantly reduced compared to full path tracing [S2, S3].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage cost after compression is around 100 MB for the base method [S2]. Hierarchical representation reduces this to approx. 60 MB [S4]. Neural network variants reduce this to 23.6 kB [S1].
-*   **Performance:** On a 2.20GHz Intel i7 (40 cores), for a 512x512 image of a BumpS scene, VRL with PMS took 53s (PSNR 34.5) vs 1s for VRL alone (PSNR 23.21) [S3]. For a Wax scene, 180s (PSNR 37.4) vs 40s (PSNR 12.28) [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Greatly reduces convergence time for high-order scattering media; integrates easily with existing algorithms (VRL, UPBP, MEMLT); higher spatial and angular resolution than concentric spheres methods [S2, S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Introduces bias; does not handle visibility or boundaries; requires precomputation per material; less effective for low-albedo materials where multiple scattering is negligible [S1, S2, S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Precomputation time is approximately 1 minute for high-albedo materials on a 40-core CPU [S2]. Rendering convergence is significantly faster than raw Monte Carlo methods (e.g., VRL with PMS took 53s vs 1s for VRL alone on BumpS scene, though VRL alone was noisy) [S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Compressed storage is ~60–100 MB [S2, S4]. Uncompressed is ~8.4 GB [S4].
+*   **Performance:** Reduces noise significantly. For the Wax scene, VRL with PMS took 180s (PSNR 37.4) vs 40s for VRL alone (PSNR 12.28) [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast convergence for high-order scattering; compatible with various algorithms (VRL, UPBP, MEMLT); handles any material type including refractive and high/low albedo [S2, S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Introduces bias; ignores visibility and boundaries; requires precomputation per material type [S1, S3].
+*   **When to use:** Scenes with high-albedo participating media where multiple scattering dominates and convergence is too slow.
+*   **When not to use:** Scenes with complex visibility/occlusion inside the medium, or where strict unbiasedness is required.
 
 ## Background
-Originated by Beibei Wang and Nicolas Holzschuch in 2017 at SIGGRAPH 2017 Talks, extending Wang et al. [2016] by adding multiple scattering from a segment instead of a point [S2]. It was implemented in the Mitsuba Renderer [Jakob 2010] for VRL and MEMLT, and in smallUPBP [Křivánek 2014] for UPBP [S2, S3].
+*   **Origin:** Introduced by Beibei Wang and Nicolas Holzschuch in 2017 at SIGGRAPH 2017 Talks [S2].
+*   **History:** It extends Wang et al. [2016] by adding multiple scattering from a segment source instead of just a point source [S2]. It was implemented in the Mitsuba Renderer [Jakob 2010] and smallUPBP [Křivánek 2014] [S2, S3].
+*   **Development:** Subsequent work included GPU implementations [S1] and neural network approximations [S1].
 
